; =====================================================================
; purl.derived.abnf
;
; DERIVED - not verbatim; ECMA-427 1st edition defines no grammar
; for the PURL string. Every rule in this file was written for this
; library from the prose of ECMA-427 Clause 5 (5 and 5.1 to 5.6.7),
; and it must never be cited as the standard's own grammar.
;
; Contains text from ECMA-427 1st edition, (c) Ecma International
; 2025, reproduced under the Ecma copyright notice; full notice in
; distilled/README.md. ("(c)" stands for the copyright sign: RFC 5234
; admits only ASCII in comments, so this file is ASCII throughout.)
; =====================================================================
;
; Source: ECMA-427, Package-URL (PURL) specification, 1st edition,
; December 2025, Ecma International TC54.
; <https://ecma-international.org/wp-content/uploads/ECMA-427_1st_edition_december_2025.pdf>
; PDF SHA-256 037180df99e7d7c26d910dc3adb3fa07c4fb787cc4bd29e43634de3802313825
;
; The 1st edition states the PURL syntax only as prose bullets in
; Clause 5. It contains no ABNF, BNF or regular expression for a PURL
; string. Its one formal artifact is the Annex A JSON Schema, which
; governs PURL type definition documents, not PURL strings; that
; schema is held verbatim beside this file as
; purl-type-definition.schema-1.0.json.
;
; Conventions. Numbers such as 5.6.3 are ECMA-427 clause numbers. A
; passage in double quotes that a comment attributes to a clause
; ("5.6.2 says: ...") is verbatim from the standard; the only change
; is that a sentence the PDF wrapped across lines is joined. Everything
; else in this file is ours. The language is ABNF (RFC 5234). The core
; rules ALPHA, DIGIT and HEXDIG (RFC 5234 Appendix B.1) are used and
; none is redefined. RFC 5234 quoted strings are case-insensitive, so
; every terminal whose case matters is written as a %x value. Checked
; with the Python package abnf 2.2.0; the commands and results are in
; README.md beside this file.

; =====================================================================
; ROOT RULES
;
; purl               A PURL string. Enforces every Clause 5 rule that a
;                    context-free grammar can enforce (under readings
;                    R1 to R5 below), and admits what Clause 5 calls
;                    non-canonical rather than invalid: upper-case
;                    letters in the type (5.6.2), and leading or
;                    trailing slashes around the namespace, the name
;                    and the subpath (5.6.3, 5.6.4, 5.6.7).
;
; canonical-shape    The strings of purl that have the canonical
;                    shape: the type in lower case and no
;                    insignificant slash. NECESSARY, NOT SUFFICIENT
;                    for canonical form; see GAPS, item G1.
;
; purl-parser-input  purl, plus the 'pkg:/' and 'pkg://' forms that
;                    5.6.1 obliges every parser to accept.
;
; Every canonical-shape string is a purl string, and every purl string
; is a purl-parser-input string.
; =====================================================================

purl              = scheme ":" purl-remainder               ; 5, 5.3

purl-remainder    = type purl-path [ "@" version ]
                    [ "?" qualifiers ] [ "#" subpath ]       ; 5, 5.3

canonical-shape   = scheme ":" canonical-type "/" [ namespace "/" ]
                    name [ "@" version ] [ "?" qualifiers ]
                    [ "#" canonical-subpath ]                ; 5, 5.3

purl-parser-input = scheme ":" *"/" purl-remainder          ; 5.6.1

; Clause 5 gives the component order as
; scheme:type/namespace/name@version?qualifiers#subpath and 5.3 says
; which separator stands between which components. Clause 5 also says:
; "A PURL shall not contain a URL Authority, i.e. there is no support
; for username, password, host and port components." No rule here has
; an authority part.

; =====================================================================
; READINGS (judgement calls; README.md lists them as well)
;
; R1 Strict percent-encoding. 5.6 says each component "shall be
;    encoded as defined in the Character encoding clause", and 5.4
;    says "The following characters shall not be percent-encoded",
;    listing "the Alphanumeric Characters", "the Punctuation
;    Characters" and "the colon ':', whether used as a Separator
;    Character or otherwise". A triplet for one of those characters
;    (%41 for A, %2E for a period, %3A for a colon) cannot come out of
;    that encoding, so this file rejects it. A reader who takes 5.4 as
;    a canonical-form rule only would accept every triplet; to get
;    that reading, define pct-octet-no-slash as 2HEXDIG.
;
; R2 Nothing empty after a separator. 5.6.5 says "The version is
;    prefixed by a '@' separator when not empty." 5.6.6 and 5.6.7 say
;    the same of '?' and '#'. This file also applies the converse (no
;    '@', '?' or '#' unless a non-empty component follows it), which
;    the text implies but does not state.
;
; R3 A name has at least one character. Table 1 marks name
;    "Required", and Clause 2 says "All required components of a PURL,
;    such as the scheme, type, and name, shall be present".
;
; R4 Lower-case scheme only. 5.6.1 says "The scheme is a constant with
;    the value "pkg"." and, unlike 5.6.2 for the type, never calls the
;    scheme case insensitive. RFC 3986 section 3.1, which 5.1 invokes,
;    does call schemes case-insensitive; a reader who imports that
;    rule would replace %x70.6B.67 by "pkg", which ABNF matches in any
;    case.
;
; R5 Where an insignificant slash may stand. 5.6.3, 5.6.4 and 5.6.7 say
;    leading and trailing slashes "are not significant". purl admits a
;    run of slashes after the type, at the namespace and name boundary,
;    after the name (so also just before '@', '?' or '#') and at both
;    ends of the subpath, but never inside the namespace or the
;    subpath, where a run would make an empty segment. So
;    pkg:npm/foobar/@1.0 has the name foobar. The purl-spec v1.0.0 test
;    suite, which is not part of ECMA-427, reads the slash before '@'
;    as a separator with an empty name after it: it expects
;    pkg:swift/github.com/Alamofire/@5.4.3 to fail. The 1st edition
;    text does not decide between the two readings.
; =====================================================================

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCHEME - 5.6.1
; 5.6.1 says: "The scheme is a constant with the value "pkg"." and "The
; scheme shall be followed by an unencoded colon ':'." The colon is the
; ":" of the root rules. For the case of the scheme, see R4.

scheme            = %x70.6B.67                ; pkg, lower case only

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; TYPE - 5.6.2
; 5.6.2 says: "The package type shall be composed only of ASCII
; letters and numbers, period '.', and dash '-'.", "The type shall
; start with an ASCII letter.", "The type shall not be
; percent-encoded." and "The type is case insensitive. The canonical
; form is lowercase."
;
; 5.6.2 sets no minimum length, so a one-letter type (pkg:a/name) is
; admitted here. The pattern that 6.1 and the Annex A schema give for
; the type of a type definition, ^[a-z][a-z0-9-\.]+$, needs at least
; two characters. README.md records this as a source defect.

type              = ALPHA *( ALPHA / DIGIT / "." / "-" )      ; 5.6.2

canonical-type    = %x61-7A *( %x61-7A / DIGIT / "." / "-" )  ; 5.6.2

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE PATH: TYPE, NAMESPACE, NAME - 5.3, 5.6.3, 5.6.4
; 5.3 says "'/' (slash) is the separator between type, namespace and
; name". 5.6.4 says "The name is prefixed by a single slash '/'
; separator when the namespace is not empty." 5.6.3 and 5.6.4 each say
; "All leading and trailing slashes '/' are not significant and should
; be stripped in the canonical form." So purl admits a run of slashes
; before and after the namespace and after the name (see R5);
; canonical-shape admits exactly one slash at each separator and none
; after the name.

purl-path         = 1*"/" [ namespace 1*"/" ] name *"/"
                                              ; 5.3, 5.6.3, 5.6.4

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; NAMESPACE - 5.6.3
; 5.6.3 says: "If present, the namespace may contain one or more
; segments, separated by a single unencoded slash '/' character." and
; "Each namespace segment shall be a percent-encoded string." When
; percent-decoded, a segment "shall not contain any slash '/'
; characters" and "shall not be empty". Under R1 both decoded-value
; rules can be decided on the encoded form: segment-char admits no
; %2F, and a segment has at least one character, each of which
; decodes to at least one octet. Whether a namespace must, may or must
; not be present is for the type definition (5.6.3: "The namespace is
; optional, unless required by the package's type definition."); see
; GAPS, item G3.

namespace         = namespace-segment *( "/" namespace-segment )
                                              ; 5.6.3
namespace-segment = 1*segment-char            ; 5.6.3

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; NAME - 5.6.4
; 5.6.4 says: "A name shall be a percent-encoded string." and "When
; percent-decoded, a name may contain any Unicode character unless the
; package's type definition provides otherwise." Unlike a segment, a
; name may therefore hold an encoded slash, %2F. For why a name is
; never empty, see R3.

name              = 1*pchar                   ; 5.6.4, R3

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; VERSION - 5.6.5
; 5.6.5 says: "The version is prefixed by a '@' separator when not
; empty.", "This '@' is not part of the version.", "A version shall be
; a percent-encoded string." and "A version is a plain and opaque
; string." See R2.

version           = 1*pchar                   ; 5.6.5, R2

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; QUALIFIERS - 5.6.6
; 5.6.6 says: "The qualifiers component shall be prefixed by an
; unencoded question mark '?' separator when not empty.", "The
; qualifiers component is composed of one or more key=value pairs.
; Multiple key=value pairs shall be separated by an unencoded
; ampersand '&'." and "A key and value shall be separated by the
; unencoded equal sign '=' character."

qualifiers        = qualifier *( "&" qualifier )              ; 5.6.6

qualifier         = qualifier-key "=" qualifier-value         ; 5.6.6

; 5.6.6 says: "The key shall be composed only of lowercase ASCII
; letters and numbers, period '.', dash '-' and underscore '_'.", "A
; key shall start with an ASCII letter." and "A key shall not be
; percent-encoded." An upper-case letter in a key is therefore
; invalid, not merely non-canonical. For uniqueness of keys, see GAPS,
; item G4.

qualifier-key     = %x61-7A *( %x61-7A / DIGIT / "." / "-" / "_" )
                                              ; 5.6.6

; 5.6.6 says: "A value shall not be an empty string" and "A value may
; contain any Unicode character and all characters shall be encoded as
; described in the Character encoding clause." See GAPS, item G7.

qualifier-value   = 1*pchar                   ; 5.6.6, 5.4

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; SUBPATH - 5.6.7
; 5.6.7 says: "The subpath string is prefixed by a '#' separator when
; not empty.", "The subpath contains zero or more segments, separated
; by slash '/'.", "Leading and trailing slashes '/' are not
; significant and should be stripped in the canonical form." and
; "Each subpath segment shall be a percent-encoded string." When
; percent-decoded, a segment "shall not contain any slash '/'
; characters", "shall not be empty" and "shall not be any of '..' or
; '.'". The three alternatives of subpath-segment admit every
; non-empty segment except one that is exactly a period or exactly two
; periods; under R1 a period is never percent-encoded, so testing the
; written form is enough.

subpath           = *"/" canonical-subpath *"/"               ; 5.6.7

canonical-subpath = subpath-segment *( "/" subpath-segment )  ; 5.6.7

subpath-segment   = segment-char-no-dot *segment-char         ; 5.6.7
                  / "." segment-char-no-dot *segment-char
                  / ".." 1*segment-char

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; CHARACTERS AND PERCENT-ENCODING - 5.2, 5.4, 5.6
; 5.6 says each component "may be composed of any of the characters
; defined in the Permitted characters clause" and "shall be encoded as
; defined in the Character encoding clause". 5.2 lists "the
; Alphanumeric Characters: A to Z, a to z, 0 to 9", "the Punctuation
; Characters: .-_~", "the Percent Character: %" and "the Separator
; Characters :/@?=&#". 5.4 makes the "allowed set" the Alphanumeric
; and the Punctuation Characters, and says "The following characters
; shall not be percent-encoded": the Alphanumeric Characters, the
; Punctuation Characters, "the Separator Characters when being used as
; PURL separators", "the colon ':', whether used as a Separator
; Character or otherwise" and "the percent sign '%' when used to
; represent a percent-encoded character". It adds: "Where the space
; ' ' is permitted, it shall be percent-encoded as '%20'."
;
; So inside a component the only characters written as themselves are
; the allowed set and the colon. Every other character of component
; data (the six other separators, the space, '+', any non-ASCII
; character) is written as triplets, one per UTF-8 octet.

allowed-char        = ALPHA / DIGIT / "-" / "." / "_" / "~"   ; 5.2, 5.4

pchar               = allowed-char / ":" / pct-encoded        ; 5.4

segment-char        = allowed-char / ":" / segment-pct-encoded
                                              ; 5.4, 5.6.3, 5.6.7

segment-char-no-dot = ALPHA / DIGIT / "-" / "_" / "~" / ":"
                    / segment-pct-encoded     ; 5.6.7

; Triplets. 5.4 applies "the percent-encoding mechanism defined in RFC
; 3986 section 2.1", which writes an octet as a percent sign and two
; hexadecimal digits. Under R1 a triplet for a character that 5.4 says
; "shall not be percent-encoded" is excluded: %30-39 (digits), %41-5A
; and %61-7A (letters), %2D, %2E, %5F and %7E (the punctuation) and
; %3A (the colon). A namespace or subpath segment also excludes %2F
; (5.6.3, 5.6.7). HEXDIG matches a-f as well as A-F: the 1st edition
; does not fix the case of hex digits, and RFC 3986 section 2.1 only
; recommends upper case to producers.

pct-encoded         = segment-pct-encoded / pct-slash         ; 5.4

pct-slash           = "%" "2" "F"             ; %2F, an encoded slash

segment-pct-encoded = "%" pct-octet-no-slash  ; 5.4, R1

pct-octet-no-slash  = ( "0" / "1" ) HEXDIG                ; %00-1F
                    / "2" ( DIGIT / "A" / "B" / "C" )     ; %20-2C
                    / "3" ( "B" / "C" / "D" / "E" / "F" ) ; %3B-3F
                    / "4" "0"                             ; %40
                    / "5" ( "B" / "C" / "D" / "E" )       ; %5B-5E
                    / "6" "0"                             ; %60
                    / "7" ( "B" / "C" / "D" / "F" )       ; %7B-7D, %7F
                    / ( "8" / "9" / "A" / "B" / "C" / "D" / "E" / "F" )
                      HEXDIG                              ; %80-FF

; =====================================================================
; GAPS: WHAT THIS GRAMMAR CANNOT SAY
;
; Stated gaps, not omissions. README.md beside this file repeats them.
;
; G1 Canonical form. canonical-shape checks only the case of the type
;    and the insignificant slashes. It cannot check the type-specific
;    case rule (a type definition with case_sensitive false means "the
;    canonical form shall be lowercased", 6.5.6, 6.6.4, 6.7.4, 6.9.4)
;    or a type's normalization_rules (6.5.7, 6.6.5, 6.7.5, 6.9.5),
;    which 6.5.7 calls "plain text, unstructured rules". Two things a
;    canonical form needs are not defined by the 1st edition at all:
;    an order for the qualifiers (no clause orders them) and the case
;    of the hex digits in a triplet. Clause 2 nevertheless says
;    "Implementations shall ensure that equivalent PURLs are
;    consistently resolved to the same canonical representation."
;
; G2 Case folding. 5.5 says "References to "lowercase" in this
;    Standard refer to the culture-invariant full case mapping defined
;    in Section 3.13.2 of the Unicode Standard." A grammar does not
;    fold case; it only admits or refuses upper-case letters.
;
; G3 Type-specific rules. A type definition can require or prohibit
;    the namespace (5.6.3, 6.5.1), require qualifier keys (6.8.1.2) and
;    narrow the characters of a component ("unless the package's type
;    definition provides otherwise", 5.6.3, 5.6.4, 5.6.5, 5.6.7; and
;    permitted_characters, 6.5.5). None of that is visible to a
;    grammar for every type. See purl-type-definition.schema-1.0.json.
;
; G4 Unique qualifier keys. 5.6.6 says "Each key shall be unique among
;    all the keys of the qualifiers component." Not context-free:
;    pkg:npm/foobar?arch=x&arch=y matches every rule in this file.
;
; G5 The 'pkg://' leniency. 5.6.1 says "PURL parsers shall accept URLs
;    where the scheme and colon ':' are followed by one or more slash
;    '/' characters, such as 'pkg://', and shall ignore and remove all
;    such '/' characters." purl-parser-input states which strings a
;    parser accepts; the duty to "ignore and remove" the slashes is a
;    transformation, and no grammar expresses one.
;
; G6 Decoded values. Under R1 the segment rules "shall not contain any
;    slash '/' characters" and "shall not be empty" (5.6.3, 5.6.7) are
;    decided on the encoded form (see NAMESPACE and SUBPATH). What no
;    rule here checks is that the octets of a component, once decoded,
;    are well-formed UTF-8 (5.4 says "the component string shall first
;    be encoded as UTF-8"). Without R1 a period could be written %2E,
;    and the subpath rule against '.' and '..' would escape the grammar
;    as well.
;
; G7 Empty qualifier values. 5.6.6 says "A value shall not be an empty
;    string: a key=value pair with an empty value is the same as if no
;    key=value pair exists for this key." purl and canonical-shape
;    enforce the first half. The second half is an equivalence that a
;    parser applies; purl-parser-input does not model it, so it
;    refuses key= as purl does.
;
; G8 Meaning, not syntax. 5.6.3: "A URL host or Authority shall not be
;    used as a namespace." 5.6.5: "A version is a plain and opaque
;    string." 5.6.7: "The subpath shall be interpreted as relative to
;    the root of the package."
;
; G9 Type names. 5.1 says special URL schemes "such as file://,
;    https://, http:// and ftp://" and VCS URLs "such git://, svn://,
;    hg://" (sic) "are not valid PURL types". Both lists are
;    open-ended, so no type name is excluded here.
; =====================================================================
